I wrote the migration files and backfill script to `/private/tmp/claude-501/-private-var-folders-8k--tk48sxd2vs-b7dml4vh-5jh0000gn-T-skillyard-eval-database-migrations-r4dOXi/646cdc43-9c69-439a-b9ee-4b7e5b285553/scratchpad/rename_users_name/`. This directory has no migration framework, so they are raw Postgres SQL. Wrap each in your tool's up/down format.

A plain `ALTER TABLE users RENAME COLUMN name TO full_name` is fast, but it breaks any pod still running code that reads or writes `name`. So this is expand/contract over several deploys.

## Deploy plan

| # | Step | Type | Rollback |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | `01_expand_add_full_name.sql` | Schema migration | Drop `full_name` |
| 2 | `02_sync_trigger.sql` | Schema migration | Drop trigger and function |
| 3 | Release A: app writes both `name` and `full_name`, reads `name` | Code deploy | Redeploy previous |
| 4 | `backfill_full_name.py` (batched, resumable) | Background job | Nothing to undo; `full_name` is re-derivable |
| 5 | `03_…not_null.sql`, then `03b_…validate…sql` (only if `name` is NOT NULL) | Schema migration | Drop constraint or column |
| 6 | Release B: app reads `full_name` | Code deploy | Redeploy Release A, which still reads `name` (kept in sync by the trigger) |
| 7 | Release C: app stops writing `name` | Code deploy | Redeploy Release B; trigger keeps `name` current |
| 8 | Wait one full release cycle. Confirm no deployed code references `name` (grep the repo, check query logs or `pg_stat_statements`). | Verification | n/a |
| 9 | `04_contract_drop_sync_trigger.sql` | Schema migration | Recreate trigger from `02` |
| 10 | `05_contract_drop_name.sql` | Schema migration, **irreversible** | Restore `name` from the backup taken before step 1 |

The trigger is what makes this safe under rolling deploys. Old pods keep writing only `name` during steps 3–6, and new pods may write only `full_name` after step 6. The trigger keeps the two columns equal in both directions, so neither version reads stale data.

## SQL

**`01_expand_add_full_name.sql`**
```sql
SET lock_timeout = '5s';
ALTER TABLE users ADD COLUMN full_name text;
```

**`02_sync_trigger.sql`**
```sql
SET lock_timeout = '5s';
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION users_sync_name_full_name() RETURNS trigger
LANGUAGE plpgsql AS $$
BEGIN
  IF TG_OP = 'INSERT' THEN
    NEW.full_name := COALESCE(NEW.full_name, NEW.name);
    NEW.name      := COALESCE(NEW.name, NEW.full_name);
  ELSIF NEW.name IS DISTINCT FROM OLD.name AND NEW.full_name IS NOT DISTINCT FROM OLD.full_name THEN
    NEW.full_name := NEW.name;
  ELSIF NEW.full_name IS DISTINCT FROM OLD.full_name AND NEW.name IS NOT DISTINCT FROM OLD.name THEN
    NEW.name := NEW.full_name;
  END IF;
  RETURN NEW;
END;
$$;
CREATE TRIGGER users_sync_name_full_name
  BEFORE INSERT OR UPDATE OF name, full_name ON users
  FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION users_sync_name_full_name();
```

**`03_constraint_full_name_not_null.sql`** (only if `name` is NOT NULL; run after the backfill reports zero nulls)
```sql
SET lock_timeout = '5s';
ALTER TABLE users ADD CONSTRAINT users_full_name_not_null
  CHECK (full_name IS NOT NULL) NOT VALID;
```

**`03b_validate_full_name_not_null.sql`**
```sql
SET lock_timeout = '5s';
ALTER TABLE users VALIDATE CONSTRAINT users_full_name_not_null;
ALTER TABLE users ALTER COLUMN full_name SET NOT NULL;  -- no scan, thanks to the validated CHECK
ALTER TABLE users DROP CONSTRAINT users_full_name_not_null;
```

**`04_contract_drop_sync_trigger.sql`**
```sql
SET lock_timeout = '5s';
DROP TRIGGER IF EXISTS users_sync_name_full_name ON users;
DROP FUNCTION IF EXISTS users_sync_name_full_name();
```

**`05_contract_drop_name.sql`**
```sql
SET lock_timeout = '5s';
ALTER TABLE users DROP COLUMN name;
```

**Backfill:** `backfill_full_name.py` walks `id` ranges in batches of 5,000 (configurable). Each batch is its own autocommit statement, with a pause between batches. It only touches rows where `full_name IS NULL`, so it can be re-run after an interruption. It exits non-z
